    "value": "Define linear maps $\\overline{\\Delta}:C/I\\to(C/I)\\otimes(C/I)$ and $\\overline{\\varepsilon}:C/I\\to k$ on the basis $u$ by $\\overline{\\Delta}(u)=u\\otimes u$ and $\\overline{\\varepsilon}(u)=1$, extending them linearly by [F5]. On $u$, coassociativity gives $\\alpha(\\overline{\\Delta}\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\\overline{\\Delta}(u)=u\\otimes(u\\otimes u)=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\overline{\\Delta})\\overline{\\Delta}(u)$, and the left and right counit composites both give $u$. Since $u$ is a basis and these are linear maps, the coalgebra identities hold on all of $C/I$. For $c_{11}$, $(q\\otimes q)\\Delta(c_{11})=u\\otimes u+0\\otimes0=u\\otimes u=\\overline{\\Delta}q(c_{11})$ and $\\varepsilon(c_{11})=1=\\overline{\\varepsilon}q(c_{11})$. For $c_{12}$, $(q\\otimes q)\\Delta(c_{12})=u\\otimes0+0\\otimes u=0=\\overline{\\Delta}q(c_{12})$ and $\\varepsilon(c_{12})=0=\\overline{\\varepsilon}q(c_{12})$. For $c_{21}$, $(q\\otimes q)\\Delta(c_{21})=0\\otimes u+u\\otimes0=0=\\overline{\\Delta}q(c_{21})$ and $\\varepsilon(c_{21})=0=\\overline{\\varepsilon}q(c_{21})$. For $c_{22}$, $(q\\otimes q)\\Delta(c_{22})=0\\otimes0+u\\otimes u=u\\otimes u=\\overline{\\Delta}q(c_{22})$ and $\\varepsilon(c_{22})=1=\\overline{\\varepsilon}q(c_{22})$. By linearity these equalities hold on all of $C$, so $q$ is a coalgebra map by [F1]."
  },
